d work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: settlement hierarchy\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  1-4 The Knossian state was disintegrated in independent regional entities (quasi-polities) centered upon a large town. . The size of these towns varies from 4 to 54 ha. Some were densely occupied while habitation in others was more dispersed, suggesting a trend toward a ruralization of the urban setting. Data points to a four-tiered hierarchy: the large town is surrounded by villages, hamlets, and farmhouses. The most important centers were Kydonia (Chania), in west Crete and Hagia Triada, in south-central Crete.    The reference to a <i>wanax</i> (king) in the inscription painted on the shoulder of some inscribed stirrup jars, produced in the area of west Crete, prompting the speculation for the presence of a "palatial" authority.  By the end of Late Minoan IIIB most settlements had suffered destruction or abandonment.\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1266, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1184})
