ded as: level 1, and work down to the private. Even in primitive societies such as simple chiefdoms it is often possible to distinguish at least two levels – a commander and soldiers. A complex chiefdom would be coded three levels. The presence of warrior burials might be the basis for inferring the existence of a military organization. (The lowest military level is always the individual soldier).\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Using the definition of 'Military Level' as a measure of hierarchical complexity within a military structure, identify the range of military levels present in the polity <polity> between <time-start> and <time-end>. Your response should reflect the levels of hierarchy from the commander-in-chief to the individual soldier and should be expressed as a JSON array in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>]."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: polity territory\n                        Variable unit: square kilometres\n                        Variable description: Talking about Social Scale, Polity territory is coded in squared kilometers.\n                    '}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': "Based on the concept of 'Polity Territory' as defined in terms of the area a polity controlled in squared kilometers, provide the range of the area <polity> governed from <time-start> to <time-end>. Please provide your answer in the format: [<start_of_range>, <end_of_range>], without the unit."}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '\n                        Variable name: settlement hierarchy\n                        Variable unit: Not provided\n                        Variable description:  levels.<br>1. Himyarite capital<br>2. Capitals of subjected kingdoms3. Larger urban areas, like the port Al-Muza4. Hagar (town)"The basis of the Middle Period social structure was the bayt "a village or clan community" although this most assuredly is not meant to convey the meaning of a village community as a bayt could be both in the countryside and within the walls of a hagar, town." Sabaean hagar consisted of bayts.<br>2. Coloniesc50 CE. "The Himyarite King Charibael had authority over distant sites in East Africa including the trading settlement at Rhapta in northern Tanzania. He leased this settlement to a merchant oligarchy from Muza who ran trade operations from the port and collected taxes on any incoming business. The Periplus explains: \'the region (Rhapta) is under the rule of the governor of Mapharitis, since by some ancient right it became subject to the Kingdom of Arabia when it was first established. The merchants of Muza hold it through a grant from the king and collect taxes from it."<br>\n                    '}], {'usage.total_tokens': 1309, 'usage.prompt_tokens': 1227})
